## 6. Problems Faced & How We Fixed Them

### P1 — USGS GeoJSON coordinate order
**Problem:** GeoJSON coordinates are `[longitude, latitude, depth]` — the opposite of what you'd expect. Naive access would silently swap lat/lon, causing all events to plot in the ocean.  
**Fix:** Explicit unpacking `g = coords; lat=g[1], lon=g[0], depth=g[2]`.

---

### P2 — ISC pipe-delimited format + error body
**Problem:** ISC doesn't support GeoJSON. Its `text` format uses pipe `|` delimiters with a `#`-prefixed header. Worse, ISC returns HTTP 200 with `"Error 404: ..."` in the body on bad queries.  
**Fix:** Strip `#` from header, parse data lines with `pd.read_csv(StringIO(...), sep='|')`. Added explicit `text.startswith("Error")` check before parsing.

---

### P3 — Cross-catalog duplicate detection was too coarse
**Problem:** First implementation used a 0.5-degree spatial window (~55 km at Kutch latitude). This collapsed legitimately separate events in the densely clustered fault zone.  
**Fix:** Replaced with vectorized haversine distance ≤ 15 km as the spatial threshold.

---

### P4 — b-value biased low by network incompleteness
**Problem:** Whole-catalog b = 0.715 — outside published range. Caused by M2–2.9 events being under-recorded before the network densified post-2001.  
**Fix:** Applied Mc = 3.0 cutoff, used Aki 1965 MLE with Utsu midpoint correction. Result: b = 0.963 ± 0.034 ✓

---

### P5 — ETAS intensity() loop was O(N×Q) per call
**Problem:** Original naive `intensity()` looped over all N history events for each of Q query points. For 1,311 events over a 2,000-point grid → 2.6M operations per optimizer call × hundreds of calls = very slow.  
**Fix:** Fully vectorized: precompute `dt_mat = t_query[None,:] - t_hist[:,None]` → apply kernel in one broadcast → sum over axis 0. 50–100× speedup.

---

### P6 — ETAS (N×N) matrix caused OOM on large synthetic catalog
**Problem:** Synthetic test generator didn't cap magnitudes (GR distribution has a heavy tail). Without a cap, one M6+ event could spawn thousands of aftershocks, creating a catalog of 300,000+ events. Precomputing the (300k × 300k) matrix tried to allocate 796 GiB → crash.  
**Fix:**  
1. Capped magnitudes at M7.5 in the GR sampler.  
2. Added a per-event aftershock count hard cap of 200.  
3. Added `_PRECOMPUTE_LIMIT = 4,000` — above this, `ETASModel` auto-switches to a chunked 512-event column loop (memory-safe, ~3× slower).

---

### P7 — numpy 2.x dropped `np.trapz`
**Problem:** `numpy >= 2.0` removed the old `np.trapz` alias.  
**Fix:** Use `np.trapezoid` (new name) or avoid trapezoidal integration entirely (closed-form Omori integrals used instead).

---

### P8 — Windows cp1252 encoding errors on all print statements
**Problem:** Python on Windows defaults to `cp1252` console encoding. Every unicode character in print statements (`→`, `✓`, `✗`, `≥`, `−`, `…`, `μ`, `α`) raised `UnicodeEncodeError`.  
**Fix:** Systematically replaced all unicode characters in print statements and DataFrame column names with ASCII equivalents (`-->`, `[OK]`, `[!!]`, `>=`, `-`, `...`, `mu`, `alpha`) across `etas_model.py`, `etas_forecast.py`, and `run_etas.py`.

---

### P9 — KS test marginal on real catalog (p=0.027)
**Problem:** ETAS time-rescaling KS test failed the 0.05 threshold on the Kutch catalog.  
**Root cause:** The Bhuj Mw7.7 event alone generates ~70% of all triggered activity. A single dominant mainshock creates a large jump in the rescaled-time series that a standard KS test flags, even when the model fits well everywhere else.  
**Status:** Known limitation. Mitigation: apply mainshock-aftershock suppression window for Mw≥6 events (next step).

